+# CASE-A: cache the parsed list, invalidate on the file's mtime
+
+## The problem, measured against the code
+
+`lookup` in `src/dictionary.mjs` does `readFileSync(...).split("\n").filter(Boolean)` then a linear `includes` on every call. `data/words.txt` is 4001 lines. A page of text is a few hundred calls, so the cost is dominated by re-reading and re-splitting the whole file hundreds of times, not by the search itself. The README promises: results are case-insensitive, `lookup` never throws on a string, throws `TypeError` on a non-string, and hand edits to the file are picked up without a restart.
+
+## Proposed approach (the simplest that meets all four promises)
+
+Keep a module-level cache: `{ mtimeMs, size, words: Set }`. On each call:
+
+1. Non-string check, unchanged.
+2. `statSync(WORDS)`; if `mtimeMs` and `size` both equal the cached values, reuse the cached `Set`.
+3. Otherwise `readFileSync`, build the `Set` with exactly the current `split("\n").filter(Boolean)` pipeline, store it with the new stat values.
+4. Return `words.has(word.toLowerCase())`.
+
+That is one small function, no new dependencies, no timers, no watchers, no changes to the data format or to how the file is edited. Results are identical by construction: the `Set` holds the very same strings `includes` was searching, so `has` answers the same as `includes` for every input, including `""` (empty lines are filtered, so `""` is still `false`) and lines with stray `\r` or spaces (they still fail to match, as they do today).
+
+Cost per call drops from "read + parse 4001 lines + linear scan" to "one `stat` syscall + one hash lookup". A `stat` is a few microseconds; the current path is milliseconds. A page goes from seconds to well under a millisecond of dictionary time. Reparsing happens only when the file actually changes, which the README says is "now and then".
+
+## Why not more
+
+- `fs.watch` picks up edits with no per-call `stat`, but it has platform quirks (events can arrive before the write finishes, on rename the watcher can follow the old inode, the handle keeps the process alive unless unref'd). All of that is machinery to save microseconds we do not need to save.
+- A sorted array with binary search saves a little memory over a `Set` but depends on the file staying sorted, which the README describes but nothing enforces. A `Set` is correct whether or not the list is sorted. 4000 short strings in a `Set` is a few hundred kilobytes.
+- Caching without any invalidation is simpler still but breaks the "picked up without restarting" promise outright.
+
+## Risks I concede
+
+- **mtime granularity.** If a save lands in the same timestamp tick as the previous read and leaves the byte count unchanged (e.g. swapping one word for another of equal length, on a filesystem with coarse mtime), the stale cache is served until the next change. Checking `size` alongside `mtimeMs` narrows this; it does not close it entirely. For a hand-edited list this is a real but very small window, and the next edit heals it.
+- **Errors while the file is being rewritten.** If an editor truncates then writes, a `stat` or read in between can see an empty file or throw `ENOENT`. Today's code throws in the same situation, so this is not a regression, but the plan should decide whether to fall back to the last good `Set` on a read error (I would: it keeps "never throws on a string" truer than it is now) and note it.
+- **Still one syscall per call.** If someone later wants zero syscalls, a watcher becomes the next step; nothing in this design blocks that, the cache and the `Set` carry over.
+- **The existing tests do not cover reload.** The first version should add a test that edits a temp copy of the list (or rewrites `data/words.txt` and restores it) and asserts the change is seen, so the invalidation is actually exercised by `npm test`. Doing that safely against the shared data file needs care in the builder plan.
