+# CASE-B: cache on the file's content, not its timestamp
+
+## Where A is right
+
+The diagnosis is correct: the milliseconds are in `split` + `filter` + `includes` (allocating 4001 strings and scanning them), not in reading ~30 KB. A `Set` is the right structure, `fs.watch` is the wrong tool, and a cache with no invalidation breaks the README's reload promise. B keeps all of that and changes one thing: what decides "the file changed".
+
+## Where A is weaker than it needs to be
+
+A invalidates on `(mtimeMs, size)`. That is a proxy for "the content changed", and A concedes the proxy has a hole: a same-size edit inside one timestamp tick. How small that window is depends on the filesystem, not on the code. APFS and ext4 report nanoseconds; exFAT rounds to 2 s, HFS+ and many SMB/NFS mounts to 1 s. The edit that slips through is swapping one word for another of the same length (`bala` -> `balb`), which is exactly the edit the README describes, and "the next edit heals it" can be days away for a list edited "now and then". The task says results must not change. A makes that true with high probability. It can be made true by construction.
+
+## Proposed approach
+
+Module-level cache `{ text, words: Set }`. On each call:
+
+1. Non-string check, unchanged.
+2. `text = readFileSync(WORDS, "utf8")`: the same read today's code does.
+3. If `text !== cached.text`, rebuild the `Set` with the same `split("\n").filter(Boolean)` pipeline and store both.
+4. Return `words.has(word.toLowerCase())`.
+
+Why this is the stronger design:
+
+- **Exact by construction.** Every call sees precisely the bytes today's `lookup` would see, and the `Set` is built from those bytes. No filesystem, editor, clock, `cp -p`, or `rsync -a` behaviour can serve a stale answer. That includes a torn write: an empty read yields an empty `Set` and `false`, which is what today's code returns, and the next call self-heals.
+- **Fewer moving parts.** No `stat`, no key tuple, no stat-then-read race, no reliance on timestamp semantics. One string comparison: O(1) when lengths differ, a memcmp when they match.
+- **Testable without timing games.** A's reload test must avoid a same-size edit landing in the same tick or it will flake, or pass for the wrong reason. B's test rewrites the file and asserts.
+
+**Answering A's best point, cost.** A: "one stat vs read + parse". B keeps the read and drops the parse, which is where the time goes. Reading 30 KB is a few syscalls and a copy; comparing it is a memcmp; both are tens of microseconds at most. A's single `stat` is cheaper, by roughly an order of magnitude, and I do not dispute that. But the target is "seconds per page" to "not the bottleneck", and 300 calls at ~30 us is ~10 ms per page, invisible next to tokenising it. Paying that to turn "almost certainly right" into "right" is the correct trade for a task whose hard constraint is unchanged results. If a later measurement shows the read matters, putting A's stat gate in front of the read is a two-line change, and it should be made then, knowingly, as a trade of exactness for microseconds, not adopted by default.
+
+## Risks I concede
+
+- **Per-call cost scales with file size.** At 4001 lines it is noise. At 100x that, A's `stat` would be right and B would be wrong. The README says the list changes by a word at a time, so size is stable, but this is the real boundary of the argument.
+- **Memory.** Holds the raw text (~30 KB) beside the `Set`. Trivial, but it is a second copy.
+- **Read errors mid-save.** Same open decision as A: `ENOENT` during an atomic-rename save throws today, while the README says "never throws on a string". I would serve the last good `Set` if one exists and rethrow otherwise, but that is a product call for the human and it is identical for both cases.
+- **Test hygiene.** Same as A: exercising reload means mutating `data/words.txt` (the path is hardcoded) and restoring it in `finally`. B only removes the timing hazard on top of that.
