+# PLAN: cache the parsed word list, invalidated by file content (CASE-B)
+
+## Decision
+
+Adopt CASE-B: a module-level cache `{ text, words: Set }` in `src/dictionary.mjs`. Each
+`lookup(word)` call keeps the non-string `TypeError` check, reads `data/words.txt` exactly as
+today, rebuilds the `Set` with the existing `split("\n").filter(Boolean)` pipeline only when the
+text differs from the cached text, and returns `words.has(word.toLowerCase())` (CASE-B.md:13-18).
+
+## Why it won
+
+- **The hard constraint is unchanged results.** A's invalidation key `(mtimeMs, size)` is a proxy
+  for "content changed", and A concedes it has a hole: a same-length word swap inside one timestamp
+  tick serves a stale answer until the next edit (CASE-A.md:28). B closes that hole by construction:
+  every call sees the same bytes today's code would see, and the `Set` is built from those bytes
+  (CASE-B.md:22). Coarse-mtime filesystems (exFAT 2 s, HFS+/SMB/NFS 1 s) and `cp -p`/`rsync -a`
+  make A's window a filesystem property, not a code property (CASE-B.md:9).
+- **Both sides agree where the time goes.** A: cost is "dominated by re-reading and re-splitting
+  the whole file" (CASE-A.md:5). B: the milliseconds are in `split` + `filter` + `includes`, not in
+  reading ~30 KB (CASE-B.md:5). B drops the parse and keeps the read; A drops both. The residual
+  difference is a `stat` versus a 30 KB read plus a string compare, tens of microseconds per call
+  and ~10 ms per 300-call page (CASE-B.md:26). B conceded A is cheaper by about an order of
+  magnitude (CASE-B.md:26); that is a trade of exactness for microseconds the task does not ask for.
+- **Fewer moving parts.** No `stat`, no key tuple, no stat-then-read race, no dependence on
+  timestamp semantics (CASE-B.md:23). No `fs.watch`, which both sides reject for its platform
+  quirks (CASE-A.md:22, CASE-B.md:5).
+- **Torn writes degrade the same way today's code does.** An empty read yields an empty `Set` and
+  `false`, matching current behaviour, and the next call self-heals (CASE-B.md:22).
+- **The reload test cannot flake.** A's test must dodge a same-size edit landing in the same tick
+  or it passes for the wrong reason (CASE-B.md:24); B's test rewrites the file and asserts.
+
+## What A got right, and where it stays in reserve
+
+A's `Set` structure, its rejection of `fs.watch`, its rejection of a sorted-array binary search
+(nothing enforces sort order, CASE-A.md:23), and its rejection of a never-invalidated cache
+(CASE-A.md:24) are all kept. A's `(mtimeMs, size)` gate is the named follow-up if a measurement
+ever shows the read itself is the bottleneck: put it in front of the read as a two-line change,
+knowingly trading exactness for microseconds (CASE-B.md:26). The boundary where that flips is a
+word list roughly 100x larger than today's 4001 lines (CASE-B.md:30); the README says the list
+changes a word at a time, so it is not expected.
+
+## Open product call for the human approver
+
+Both cases raise the same question and defer it (CASE-A.md:29, CASE-B.md:32): if `readFileSync`
+throws mid-save (e.g. `ENOENT` during an atomic-rename write), today's code throws, while the
+README says `lookup` never throws on a string (CASE-A.md:5). Recommended default, as both authors
+would choose: **serve the last good `Set` if one exists, rethrow otherwise.** This makes the
+"never throws on a string" promise truer than it is now and changes no result for any input the
+current code answers. If the approver prefers strict parity with today's behaviour, drop the
+try/catch and let it throw; the rest of the plan is unaffected.
+
+## Smallest first version
+
+Change only `src/dictionary.mjs` and the test file. No new dependencies, no change to
+`data/words.txt`, its format, or how it is edited (README.md).
+
+1. Add a module-level `let cache = null;` holding `{ text, words }`.
+2. In `lookup(word)`:
+   - Keep the existing non-string check and `TypeError` unchanged.
+   - `const text = readFileSync(WORDS, "utf8");` (the same read as today), wrapped per the
+     product call above.
+   - `if (cache === null || cache.text !== text) cache = { text, words: new Set(text.split("\n").filter(Boolean)) };`
+   - `return cache.words.has(word.toLowerCase());`
+3. Do not touch the split/filter pipeline, the path constant, or any exported name.
+
+## Checks for the first version
+
+All must pass under `npm test`; the last one is a bench, run once and recorded in the PR
+description rather than asserted in CI.
+
+1. **Existing suite unchanged and green.** No existing assertion is edited.
+2. **Result parity.** For a fixed sample (a known word in its listed case, the same word in
+   upper/mixed case, a word not in the list, `""`, a word with a trailing space, a word with a
+   trailing `\r`), the new `lookup` returns exactly what the old implementation returns. The old
+   implementation may be kept in the test as an inline reference function built from the same
+   `readFileSync/split/filter/includes` pipeline so the comparison is literal.
+3. **Non-string still throws `TypeError`.** `lookup(42)`, `lookup(null)`, `lookup(undefined)`.
+4. **Reload is seen without restart.** Read `data/words.txt`, append a word that is not present
+   (assert `false` before), write the file, assert `true`; then perform a same-length swap of that
+   word for another (e.g. change its last character) immediately, with no sleep, assert the old
+   spelling is `false` and the new one `true`; restore the original bytes in `finally`. The
+   same-length, no-sleep step is the one A's design cannot promise (CASE-A.md:28) and is what
+   makes this test meaningful for B (CASE-B.md:24).
+5. **Repeated calls hit the cache.** After one call, a second call with the file unchanged must
+   not rebuild the `Set`; assert via a counter on a wrapped `Set` constructor or by checking the
+   cached object identity through a test-only export, whichever the builder finds least invasive.
+   If neither is clean, drop this check and rely on the bench.
+6. **Bench (not asserted).** Time 300 calls before and after on the 4001-line list. Expect the
+   after figure well under 100 ms in total (CASE-B.md:26 predicts ~10 ms). If the after figure is
+   dominated by the read rather than the compare, record it and open the `(mtimeMs, size)` gate
+   from CASE-A as a follow-up; do not add it in this version.
